<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="mpo_700">

    <!--Gazebo Wheel Macro-->
    <xacro:macro name="gazebo_mpo_700_wheel" params="
      wheel_prefix
      ">
        <gazebo reference="${wheel_prefix}_link">
            <mu1>0.05</mu1>
            <mu2>0.05</mu2>
            <!-- Wheel contact was kp = 1e5, kd = 10. Neobotix's world used to
                 carry fifteen "wall sticker" models whose plane collisions ignore
                 their pose in ODE, i.e. fifteen extra floor planes stacked on the
                 real one, which made the wheel contact roughly sixteen times
                 stiffer and better damped by accident. With the stickers removed
                 the base bounced on its own wheel springs at about 8 Hz and never
                 settled (0.13 to 0.17 m/s of ground-truth velocity while parked,
                 sunk 21 mm, arm and controllers active).

                 kd = 10 was ~0.3 percent of critical damping for a 130 kg robot
                 on four such contacts. kd = 1000 alone calmed the bare base but
                 not the base with the arm held by gazebo_ros2_control, whose
                 per-step joint teleports keep kicking a soft contact. Raising kp
                 to 1e6 as well puts the contact back in the regime the stacked
                 planes gave (ERP 0.91, CFM 9e-5 at the 10 ms step).

                 No colon followed by a space in this comment, on purpose.
                 gazebo_ros2_control passes the whole URDF to controller_manager
                 as a command-line parameter override that rcl parses as YAML,
                 and that sequence makes the parse fail, so controller_manager
                 never starts and the arm hangs limp. Run
                 scripts/check_urdf_for_ros2_control.py after editing. -->
            <kp>1000000.0</kp>
            <kd>1000.0</kd>
        </gazebo>
    </xacro:macro>

</robot>